Warren Killian <killianw@wishard.edu> writes:

> I work for a mid-sized county hospital.  Our IS dept. is considering
> going to Red Hat Linux for a web serving platform.  We have a Dec Alpha
> machine that will become avialable to us soon.  My question is this. 
> Would we be better to use the Dec Alpha machine (for free) or try to put
> together a machine with an Intel processor?

Alpha is 64-bit, Intel (so far) is 32. There's also other differences,
for example with floating points. Alpha Linux and Alpha versions of
associated software are also less used than x86 Linux. The differences
in architecture and the number of users leads, in the probabilistic
sense, to relative unstability of an Alpha solution compared to an x86
solution. 

In Red Hat Linux for Alpha (originally 5.2, now 6.0/6.1), I have had
extra problems (nonexistent with Intel) at least with GNOME and its
libraries, scwm, xemacs, ntp and kernel timekeeping, numerical
extensions of Python, ghostview, Accelerated X, stability of X11
connections over the network, and Netscape. Netscape was not available
at first, and the Tru64 version has partly a different set of bugs
than the Intel one. 

I haven't used a web server in Alpha, nor tried other distributions
than Red Hat. Red Hat is rumoured to be a bad choice for Alpha,
altough I think many people haven't tested the latest distribution
(6.1). And things like apache (if you're going to use it), perl, and
python are quality software which have been widely used in 64-bit
architectures as well.

You could try the Alpha, maybe with SuSe or Debian, and switch to
Intel if things don't work. Changes are still that you won't have any
extra problems.

-- 
Janne



