Warren Killian <killianw@wishard.edu> wrote:
> Hello List,

> I work for a mid-sized county hospital.  Our IS dept. is considering
> going to Red Hat Linux for a web serving platform.  We have a Dec Alpha
> machine that will become avialable to us soon.  My question is this. 
> Would we be better to use the Dec Alpha machine (for free) or try to put
> together a machine with an Intel processor?

If the Alpha machine is actually free then it is worth giving it a go.
As a very rough guess, most Alpha machines will have better quality
hardware and be more reliable at the hardware level than most intel
machines. For example, many Alpha systems will do error correction on
their memory, very few intel machines do... maybe you have a nuclear
medicine unit next door?

If you have to pay for your machine then buy a cheap intel compatible
because you don't need the 64 bit capabilities of the Alpha just for web
serving and things like Cyrix are very cheap and will still easily handle
your web server requirements. Don't get talked into buying a graphics
card or a sound card or speakers or a printer to go with that -- get
a motherboard with built-in video, it won't be splendiferous but it will
be enough to run a console which is all you need.

> But how about third party software?  If we need to purchase some nice
> Linux application from some other company, would it also run fine on
> Linux/Alpha.

> If we'll be purchasing software from some other company (and I'm sure we
> will at some point), would we be safer to just stick with Linux running
> on an Intel processor?

I seriously suggest that you think about how much you would feel is
a reasonable price for third-party software and pay that money to a
consultant for the job of getting the thing up and running using free
software. Make sure that they leave documentation as to what they have
done and how you can tweak the bits later on. In most cases the
free-software works better and it is a longer term solution because
you get source code along with it.

Admittedly, it is always difficult to find a consultant who really
knows what they are doing and gives you value for your dollar... but then
again, how much value do you get buying third party software when they
offer no more warranty than free software and they will make you pay
for a support call when you attempt to report any bugs?

> We do have some unix expertise in house, but in general we're not
> gurus.  We'd like to go with the "safest" option.  Would the safest bet
> be Alpha, Intell, or does it really matter?

Use your inhouse experts to make a reasoned choice about who they
pay to do the job properly, have at least one inhouse person work close
enough to the consultant that they have a good idea how it fits together.

If you are not gurus then buying an off-the-shelf package offer that
looks like it fixes everything will most often be a waste.

	- Tel




