We are encountering problems when compiling our CFD code with either gcc or
Compaq ccc on a Microway-installed RedHat 6.0 system (164LX). To my
understanding, some items (variables, functions) are missing in the system
libraries. Although the compilation stage itself runs fine, we get the
following error messages when linking shared or non-shared versions of our
code with either compiler:

gcc :
collect2: ld terminated with signal 11 [Segmentation fault]

gcc -static :
collect2: ld terminated with signal 11 [Segmentation fault]
/home/kermadec.users/gallez/NumLab/lib/libNumLabDec.a(managerMemory.)(.lita+
0x18): undefined reference to `_iob'

ccc :
collect2: ld terminated with signal 11 [Segmentation fault]

ccc -non_shared :
/home/kermadec.users/gallez/NumLab/lib/libNumLabDec.a(managerMemory.)(.lita+
0x10): undefined reference to `fputs'
/home/kermadec.users/gallez/NumLab/lib/libNumLabDec.a(managerMemory.)(.lita+
0x18): undefined reference to `_iob'

We first compile all our source files but the main program and build a
library (libNumLabDec.a), then compile the main program and try to link it
against libNumLabDec.a. Interestingly, Compaq ccc introduces an additional
depency (fputs); but in any case, the variable '_iob' is missing.

There is a linker in /usr/bin/ which comes from a binutils package:

deepflow:>rpm -qf /usr/bin/ld
binutils-2.9.1.0.23-4
deepflow:>

but neither compilers seem to make use of it:

