This is likely because that this rpm package is really a "cheat".
It simply includes .tar.gz and the whole work is done in
various pre- and post- scripts with possibly unintended consequences;
especially if will try to uinstall and you have some conflicts.
Type 'rpm -q --scripts -p netscape-4.1-1.alpha.rpm' if you want to
see these.  This way of packaging totally circumvents, obviously,
all rpm integrity and consistency checks and one could likely
use 'shar' instead with better effects.

I have no idea what 'alien' does with these various scripts.  From
the above apparently nothing much and this is likely a good thing.

: (7) running as a general user, netscape under kde works 
:     fine, but only the *FIRST TIME* it is executed and 
:     then exited.

I cannot say very much about that beyond that in my personal experience
KDE is too buggy on Alpha to be of any serious use.  Both what I
recompiled myself with glibc2 and the stuff from RH 6.1/glibc2.1
is consistently crashing my video hardware, taking away a keyboard
and a mouse in the process, and doing some other "interesting" visual
effects.  With my stuff "randomly", with that from 6.1 "on demand".
After such incident I can only logon via network and 'shutdown -r now'
or the whole machine will lock up pretty soon.  How this is relevant
to your Netscape experience I cannot tell beyond that KDE is obviously
at this moment quite sick.

  Michal




