I only change milo when I change major OS levels.  You may need to
change milo, but try it first without upgrading.  The current version
of milo is obtainable from places like

ftp://metalab.unc.edu/pub/Linux/distributions/redhat/redhat-6.1/alpha/milo/

or wherever you can get the one from debian. Changing milo's config could
be necessary if you said something like

boot sda1:/vmlinux2.2.13.gz root=/dev/sda1

in the OS configuration in BIOS which is why I use a symlink to the kernel as in

boot sda1:/boot/vmlinux.gz root=/dev/sda1

where /boot/vmlinux.gz -> /vmlinux2.2.14.gz

That's what I use, in any case.  Best of luck.

Regards,

Tony


