In article <86kfje$flg$1@nnrp1.deja.com>,
  don_sharp@my-deja.com wrote:
> from SRM I typed
>
> >>> boot dka400 -file kernels/generic.gz -flags
> "root=/dev/sr0"
<snip>

Ok the linux device for the cdrom *is* /dev/scd0, not sr0, at least in
RH 6.1 so try:
[From SRM]
boot dka4 -fi kernels/generic.gz -fl "root=/dev/scd0"

> I chose /dev/sr0 because after a few fruitless
> attempts with /dev/scd0 I noticed that it was
> reporting the CD it saw during boot as /dev/sr0
eek.   On my system the device you had to specify from SRM was
/dev/scd0.  I can tell you that I could *never* get it to listen to my
flags from the ABOOT prompt (on the cd)...

> I have concluded that the boot line is not being
> taken in its entirety or something is slightly off
> and it is going back to its default.
When I screwed up, and then tried it again from the ABOOT prompt it
*did* ignore my flags...

> I tried from the aboot prompt but I didn't put
> parentheses around the arguments.
Hehe, maybe thats why it didnt work for me?

> I would also like instructions of how to activate
> the fdisk so that I can modify the BSD labelled
> partition information.

I believe what I did was boot the CD, and choose an Upgrade, at which
point it mounts the hard drive (I *believe* as /mnt/sysimage, and then
sub partitions under that)...  What you do is as it starts to access the
HD is hit alt-f2 and then cd into the directory ?/mnt/sysimage? while
its still mounted....  What happens is that the loader tries to unmount
it, but since you are in it...

What this *really* does for you is leave the special file /tmp/sda
there...  The problem is that the /dev filesystem is on the cdrom, and
that kind of makes it a little too static to adapt to your hardware
config, and for the life of me I couldn't find anything to create the
device file on the cdrom...

At this point (hit alt-f1 and make sure you have the error) type fdisk
/tmp/sda (assuming thats the drive) then hit 'b' for BSD style
disklabels, and create your partitions.

>>>>> not I said */tmp* not /dev<<<<<

Some warnings:
- It doesnt 'default' to anything correctly
  - It wants to start everything at the beginning of the drive
  - It does not automatically pick the partition 'number' for you
(remember they are a,b,c,d,e..)
- No matter WHAT it tells you, +sizeM *doesnt work* you're going to have
to do the math yourself

Ok now you need to create the first partition offset from the beginning
of the drive.. I started on cyl 10, that is on a 2.1gb drive, and was
much much bigger than I needed, so I wasted like 8mb...

Also I did two things I recommend:
- made a small partition that mounts as /boot
- If you want an install with utils and kernel source, and gnome (or
KDE) then you need at least a 1 gig /usr filesystem, I made mine a
seperate partition.

Also watch the partition types, the numbering is nothing like dos
disklabels..

From there you reboot and go into DiskDruid and add the mount points,
and proceed with the install.

Don't forget to install ABOOT on sda before trying to boot from the hard
drive...

Goodluck Don...  I swear my experience with RH 6.1 on alpha makes me
almost want to run NT on the thing....

--Adam (who _really_ does not care for NT) Auerbach

