Hm, when I start my "bog standard" RH 6.1 (I really wanted to
see what works and what does not before I start tweaking things)
in "Control Center", when I click on "Window Manager", I see
the following in a window on the right:
         Enlightenment(Current)
         twm
         Window Maker
and I can pick a new one with a mouse and "Try->OK".  There is also
"Add" button which makes possible to add window managers which are
not picked up automatically.  It is true that "Control Center" likes
to bomb when saving switched manager configurations but it saves
them beforehand :-), so loging out and login is restores sanity
and gets a new window manager running.

On my "hack box", when I added ICEwm it showed up in a "Control Center"
without my manual intervention.  I do not remember about sawmil.
I also know that fvwm2 works fine as a window manager for Gnome altough
I would probably would have rewrite its configuration file a bit and
make windows decorations more discrete.  Things work now but are
visually not very appealing.  'twm' works as well but it may be too
frugal for some tastes. :-)  Advantages of things like ICEwm or sawmill
are that they are Gnome-aware and behave tad differently when used
for Gnome then when run "stand-alone".

For the later on my "hack box" I actually dropped in a few one line
scripts into /etc/X11/gdm/Sessions/ and added corresponding entries
into /etc/X11/xdm/Xsession.  If you will look at these files it is
pretty obvious what to do.  Actually one of entries is set to look
at my ~/.Xclients and execute whatever it will find there.  The net
effect of these is that new menu entries show up "by itself" in
"Session" menu of gdm and I can pick up a number of different
configuration with a mouse without changing anything on my computer.

  Michal







