Difficult to say what's going on but there are some things you might look at
to find out.

a) execute the command "strings /usr/bin/passwd | grep '/'  " and look through
the output for the pathnames of files you can later check to ensure that they
are still available to the system.  The passwd command usually depend s on a
shared library and its path will be shown in the ouput of the command given
above.

b) execute the command sum -r /bin/passwd and then verify the checksums are
the same as those on a similar system.

c) Make sure the permissions of the passwd executable are correct particularly
that any SUID or SGID bits are set.  There should be at least a SUID or SGID
bit set on the executable and also that the user and group ownerships are
correct for your system.  On linux Red Hat 6.x the path is /usr/bin/passwd:
-r-s--x--x   1 root    root    /usr/bin/passwd
and on UnixWare 7.x it is
-r-sr-sr-x   1 root    sys     /bin/passwd
and on SCO Open Server 5.x it is
---x--s--x   1 bin     auth    /bin/passwd

The purpose of giving you the non linux examples was to show that the
permissions may be different between whoever distributes your version of
unix.  Never expect them to be all the same.

Perhaps removing a rpm forced the removal of a shared library. I hope this
helps.

Regards,
Alan Beall.

