Hey all.. appreciate the input...  I *did* get it working...  I later
found some doc on alphalinux.org that would have made it easier.... Just
going on random bits of info I gleened from various (often old) doc this
is what I did:

- blew out the initial install... (aka how you fix Windows ;)

- used a combination of alt-f2 and being a mean bastard to the
installation program to make it leave the special file /tmp/sda there
(Im so special...   You have to start an install, then as it mounts the
drives to look around you [timing can be a little tricky here] you cd
into the ?/mnt/sysimage? fs [use 'mount' to doublecheck where it mounts
the current partitions] and then since you are in the mounted fs, it
cannot umount it)

->>>>> used fdisk to (ugg) repartition the drive using *BSD* disklabels
(the fdisk support seems to need a little improvement here BTW)...  Im
not really worried about sharing my hard drive with another OS, so its
no big deal..

->>>>> started partition 'a' (not 1, this is a bsd disklabel afterall)
at block 10 (much farther from the start of the drive than I needed)
- reinstalled redhat

BUT

->>>>>switched to the second virtual console and mounted the hard drive
and installed ABOOT before rebooting...

Now when the machine boots into the SRM console I just boot dka2 (in my
case) with the appropriate -fi -fl flags, and viola...


I would *never* have figgured this out just from the PurpleHelmet
materials  (maybe thats my deficiency, but...)

BTW Im not positive I needed BSD disklabels and ABOOT as is, but hey it
works now, and that mount bit will let you fdisk your drives at least
(Disk Druid is EVIL).

I have a new problem now, but Ill start a new thread on that...

Thanks Again, hope this helps someone...

--Adam Auerbach


