David Hocking <dhocking@ihug.com.au> wrote:
: I'm running debian with 2.0 kernel on a DEC Multia but had problems
: upgrading some programs. Someone told me that upgrading my kernel to 2.2.x
: would fix the problem.
: I can only boot in single user mode.
: I grabbed the 2.2.14 kernel (latest) and did
:     make config
:     make dep
:     make clean
: these worked fine.
: However when i went to do "make bzImage" i get an error message that says
: these's no rule to make bzImage.
: My clock is all over the place... leading to warnings about future dates
: when compiling. This couldn't be the problem could it?
: Any suggestions?

Yes :-). "bzImage" exisits only for x86-platforms. On any other
platform you have to do "make boot" to compile the kernel.

So, a typical commandline could be:
	make clean dep boot modules modules_install

Regards,
  Heiner
