Newsgroups: comp.os.linux.alpha
Originator: murray@quatre.pa.dec.com


> whenever I boot the new kernel, the system time-of-day clock
> jumps ahead to the year 2048. 

I've seen the reverse problem with Tru64 on Miatas.  I don't know
the whole story.  I assume it's a mixup between the console
firmware and the OS that got out of sync on the Y2K fixups.

In /etc/sysconfig/clock there is an ARC=true|false option.
You might see if that is set wrong.  (sysconfig.txt says
it's a 42 year offset so that doesn't sound right.)

If you aren't occasionally running other OSes, you can just
set it correctly from Linux and let the console think the clock
is off in the reverse direction.

I'm surprised this isn't in a FAQ/HOWTO yet, but I haven't seen
it in one.

