They might not have been designed that way, but at least two of the 433a's i've
seen didn't have the Q-Logic scsi chip fitted at all (though the pads were
there) and were NT bios only as shipped. Officially, Digital did not support SRM
console on the -a machines and prior to V5 Tru64, scsi was a requirement for
installation. You can of course get round it by installing a Q-Logic pci scsi
controller and reflashing the bios with the -au version.

> The 3000 5300
> and 7300 are NT only(factory installed) $soft received money for each one
> shipped..

Ok, but are there machines that are equivalent in hardware terms, but just have
a different label on the front, like the 433 ?. 

> 
> The a/au were for tracking royalties, a's had NT Factory installed($soft),
> au's had unix/vms ($soft received no royalties).

So that's why they did it. I always thought it was some cynical targeted
marketing plot :-)...

> 
> XL's were NT only, $soft received money for each one shipped. The problem
> here is the XL never had an SRM "RELEASED" for it. That is being looked at
> though, for linux-only installations with vms and unix support disabled.
>

I've been looking into getting SRM bios into XL machines. The XL backplane /
motherboard and cpu module look identical to the Alphaserver 400, which does
come with SRM and ARC bios. I think the flash device may be smaller on the XL,
but there should be enough space to load one or the other, if not both. 

There are gotchas, such as SRM console is needed for AS400 bios update on the
latest bios releases, so you can't just plug an AS400 bios disk into an XL and
update. One approach may be to bootstrap the process by starting with a much
older bios version which did allow update from ARC console, then using that to
load the latest revision. There are other ways, such as splitting the AS400 bios
file into it's 3 components, then using the update utility to load SRM only, but
need more info before I can try that. My guess is that it's been done years ago
within Digital, probably CSS group or similar, but how to get the information
?...


Chris



