In /etc/rc.d/init.d/halt

add a   "-i" to the command="halt"  and command="reboot"

example :     command="halt -i"

At the end of the script, you will see an "eval   $command  -i -d -p

for some reason, that -i does not seem to get passed???

The restore messages for srm are normal, they are kernel debug messages.
The SRM code that uses the sdram memory while executing is relocated by the
kernel from low to high memory. When the kernel shuts down, it tries to
restore the console to its original sdram memory location.  What happens, is
if there is activity from the network card during this restore, a machine
check is generated, because the handlers are not where it expects them...

the -i tells the kernel to shutdown all network activity and flush the
interrupts..  (basically)

JJ

