Try this:

Boot from the CD or floppy. Remember that Alt-F2 switches to the second
terminal? And that's how you get to use swriteboot? The last trick to pull
before re-booting is to cd /boot, look for the uncopressed kernel (an epic
name such as vmlinux-2.2.05a...), and cp the critter to just vmlinux (or vm,
or whatever).

After re-booting, halt the machine so you get the the SRM. Type the
following:

set os_type UNIX
set bootdef_dev dka0  - use "sho dev" on SRM to inventory your machine
set boot_file /boot/vmlinux  - your kernel name and path
set boot_flags "root=/dev/sda0"  -   change sda0 to whatever linux assigns
your boot disk

You can type "sho b*" at the SRM console to verify I have all the variable
names correct. Compaq Web site helps, too - they only documet the DS10 and
greater, but most of it still applys. And don't forget
http://www.alphalinux.org too!

