> Dirk Grunwald                    Assoc. Prof, Univ. of Colorado at Boulder,

So, do you guys have someone there to do the assembly language coding
that it would take to actually _use_ the Athlon's floating point performance?
I haven't heard of a compiler for Linux that even uses the PIII speedups
(which are minimal since MMX is integer-only).  At least if you go with
Alpha, you can get optimizing compilers.

At the testdrive site, they only have one up1000, and it runs FreeBSD
and has no access to the fast compilers, so you're stuck with gcc which
knows nothing about alpha optimization.

I just think it's a hell of a lot easier to port code when you don't have
to code in assembly language.  Call me crazy...

It would be nice if someone wrote a compiler for Athlon, but that takes
time and I certainly wouldn't expect one soon.

BTW, I only see about a 75% speedup (MHz for MHz) going from ev56 to ev6,
does the ev67 go faster still?  For me, 75% justifies the cost of going
to the 264 boxes, so it's not like I really care.

Well, anyway, just some things to take into consideration before buying
a bunch of boxes.

Regards,

Tony



