I recall starting at digital and trying to explain that we did not make
all 
those things that said digital on them.
Digital watches and so on.
So, we called it DEC.
I wonder if going back to digital is partially responsible for the
demise.
I wonder if hiring all those folks from honeywell when it started
closing down
added to the demise.
I wonder if hiring all the RCA folks added to the demise.
Hmmm. Nope, I think it was the basic lack of marketing acumen as
Terry/Charlie
said.  Ken and Stan used to say engineering had to meet with the
customers
to find out how their designs worked.  That seemed to help us do some
fair stuff.
But we certainly were not marketeers.  I know I held out hope for the 8
to 
continue when the world was moving to 32 bits.  I know I held out how
for the
10 to continue when the world was being overwhelmed with vax and
interdata and SEL
and then GOULD/SEL, ...

Sometimes I wonder if the results would have been different if the
6500/6502/6800
had been the corp micro of choice by dec.  Instead of the 8080.

Like speculating about CP/M  I guess.....
bob



