Right now I use NS 4.7 for Tru64 UNIX on my Debian GNU/Linux 2.2 Alpha
system. If you have access to the Tru64 libs you can install them and
run NS. If you have access to the libs and need help installing them,
you may contact me privately using the address below.

I've been told the binutils in Debian GNU/Linux 2.2 (potato/unstable)
includes a patch that was posted to axp-list@redhat.com a few months
back. This patch allows use of the famed -taso flag like the one used in
Tru64's compilers. For those who are unaware this allows compilation of
32-bit binaries, something that was previously not possible. It may now
be possible to _ask_ Netscape for a native Alpha Linux browser. In
theory Netscape should be able to take the same source used for the
Tru64 version and recompile it on a Debian Alpha system. (someone
correct me if I'm wrong.) Netscape already has a native SPARC, PowerPC,
and x86 Linux browsers, I don't see why they couldn't produce an Alpha
Linux browser.


