From: Dirk Grunwald <grunwald@dirk-linux.cs.colorado.edu>
Subject: Does the UP1000 really suck?
Date: 2000/01/14
Message-ID: <qxpogaoxz8q.fsf@dirk-linux.cs.colorado.edu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 572670692
X-Trace: 14 Jan 2000 11:52:07 -0700, dirk-linux.cs.colorado.edu
Organization: University of Colorado
Newsgroups: comp.os.linux.alpha,comp.sys.dec


We're looking into refreshing our Alpha farm. We can not continue to
pay the high per-cpu price for Digital Unix for what are, effectively,
batch systems for high performance computing and simulation work.

So, we're switching to Linux.

We priced UP2000s, DS20s and other systems. The UP1000 is priced such
that you don't feel too bad for not just buying Athlon's.

However, a vendor told us that the UP1000 motherboard isn't as
performant as the e.g., UP2000 or even a DS10. Allegedly, the problem
is the AMD Irongate chipset and the 64-bit path to memory. He claimed
that we'd be better off buying a 600Mhz EV5.

On our integer workloads, we see a ~2.3x speedup moving from an EV5 to
EV6 at the same clock speed. On FP code, we see a ~3.7x speedup. If
the only affordable option is DS10's at 466Mhz, then I'm going to
argue for 800Mhz Athlons instead, since the FPU performance should be
similar to a 400Mhz EV6 and the integer performance should be much
better than the DS10.

However, a 700Mhz UP1000 for ~$3500 is very appealing. If they don't suck.

Anyone have one? Comparisons? www.spec.org doesn't have a peep about
them.



-- 
Dirk Grunwald                    Assoc. Prof, Univ. of Colorado at Boulder,




