>for the computer, it's tough to justify spending $39 at Starship just for a
>floppy drive.


When you turn the machine on, check the green LED that is next to the
ethernet connector on the back. Wait about 10 seconds after powering
up. If the LED flashes 14 or more times, your Multia doesn't like the
RAM you put in. Also, from what I've seen, the Multia doesn't care
what bank you put the RAM in as long as it's paired in that bank.

A long shot would be that your monitor isn't syncing properly with the
onboard TGA adapter. If you look inside your multi just behind the
floppy drive bracket, there are 2 rows of 3 jumpers, two of which are
covered at any time. If you hold the Multia with the front facing you,
cover the bottom two rows. That will set the TGA to 60Hz refresh.

A very remote but possible cause of last resort would be that your
Multia's flash memory was corrupted. You can do a failsafe load of the
ARC and SRM firmware. It's very easy to do but requires making some
firmware floppies and switching a jumper that's under the floppy drive
bracket. The www.starshipcorp.com webpage also has complete details on
how to do this.

See www.starshipcorp.com/troubleshooting.htm for more details.

Good luck...if you have other questions, let me know....

-Anthony



