Dirk Grunwald <grunwald@dirk-linux.cs.colorado.edu> writes:

> However, a vendor told us that the UP1000 motherboard isn't as
> performant as the e.g., UP2000 or even a DS10. Allegedly, the problem
> is the AMD Irongate chipset and the 64-bit path to memory. He claimed
> that we'd be better off buying a 600Mhz EV5.

The first part is true; the stream number is about 1/2 that of a DS10.

However, as you know, many codes are cachable enough that this is not
a huge performance hit. And any vendor who glosses over this
complicated issue deserves to not get your business -- they should
instead offer to let you benchmark, or at least give you the stream
numbers instead of nebulous slime.

> On our integer workloads, we see a ~2.3x speedup moving from an EV5 to
> EV6 at the same clock speed. On FP code, we see a ~3.7x speedup.

That's surprising. I saw only 2x on FP on a wide range of codes. Your
scaling is even bigger than the "stream" memory bandwidth increase
(3x). Is it a cache effect? In which case, you should consider the
cache sizes on these various machines.

> However, a 700Mhz UP1000 for ~$3500 is very appealing. If they don't suck.

Supposedly, 2 cpu UP2000s list for ~ $7500. There's more than one way
to do it. But the Athlon system probably generates more SPECfp/$$, if
you are doing a straight throughput computation using individual cpus.

On the other hand, a 466 MHz DS10 for $3000 is a clear loser, since 2x
the memory performance probably doesn't make up for the slower cpu on
your actual problems.

-- g

