Christopher Browne (cbbrowne@knuth.brownes.org) wrote:
: In article <nw9e4.7234$%K1.394994@tw12.nn.bcandid.com>, Rockmover
: wrote:
: >
: >Booting from MILO and using the
: >
: >    boot hda1:vmlinux.gz root=/dev/hda1
: >
: >or hda0 or hda3 doesn't work (I'm still booting from the MILO Disk)
: >
: >any suggestions???  I'm so close but yet so far away! :)
: >
: >I can "ls hda1:" and see the files but I don't see a "vmlinux.gz" should
: >there be one????

: You're *nearly* there.

: You need to determine which file is the actual kernel file.

Actually you don't.  An installation procedure from RH 6.1 writes
/etc/milo.conf file for you.  Try to type at milo prompt 'b hda1:'
('hda1' in your case, for others this depends on an installation
partition).  This should produce a list of labels for your boot images -
just after a fresh installation you will see only 'linux' on it.  Type
'linux' and you should be on your way.

It is not out of question that this is even documented somewhere
but I did not bother to look yet. :-)

Moreover today I found that when you are installing from SRM then
/etc/aboot.conf is also written.  There is this other small detail that
swriteboot and abootconf were not run, or at least not that they
succeeded, so a hard disk right after an installation was not yet
bootable.  Still in any case you can always boot from the same CD you
were installing everything but simply you will tell that your root
partition is that one freshly installed file system from a hard drive;
and once you booted you can correct all small oversights.

Not that installing on SRM machine is for the faint in heart novices.
Sigh! I cannot see anywhere in this spiffy graphic installer from 6.1 a
place to write a required "BSD-style disklabel".  Somebody mentioned
somewhere an "Expert" choice.  I am afraid that I cannot find any.
Besides "Disk Druid" is hopelesly broken and fit only for the most
casual use where it may screwup as well.  If you already wrote disklabel
(with 'fdisk', for example) then it will be honored, luckily enough.

BTW - you can get still "an old console-style" installer when you add
'text' to your boot flags.  This may be required if your graphics card
does not want to play.

  Michal




