Hi,

below follows a thread forwarded to me on the subject.

Is there a problem with the stacksize setting for the
kernel running on the Alpha UP2000 motherboard based system?

I am currently running kernel 2.2.12.

When I set the stacksize to something huge, i.e. unlimited,
like I do on all my Pentium-based systems, I can run big
number-crunching programs.

On the Alpha though, the system seems to come to a huge
slowdown every once in a while...
top seems to take about 20-40% of the CPU!!!
everything is dead-slow, including shutting down.

After I reboot, I am able to start an analysis and run it.
After it has run, the system seems normal for a while, then
after gzip and various trivial tasks, it starts to slow
down again and then I have to reboot, etc.

I tried re-setting the stacksize to something lower, about
512M, but I should probably set it down to about 8M (as
it is by default?)

The problem with that is I can then no longer run some
graphics applications.

My questions:

1.)  How does stacksize affect maximum size of arrays in my
programs, i.e. I have some big (~10000000) arrays, and stacksize
seems to limit this.

2.)  Is there a way around this, i.e. something I can put inside
my program, that is more efficient and can handle a lower
stacksize.

3.)  The x386 kernel seems to be ok with stacksize=unlimited.  Is
there a way to fix the alpha kernel, perhaps manipulating some
of the .h files within the kernel source code?



Help, my machine hasn't worked quite right for a long time now!!


Thank you,


AG




