Dr. Unk (dr_unk@hotmail.com) wrote:
: As far as I know, I've never seen a file called milo.conf

That can be true.

: (only lilo.conf on
: x86's).

Let's fix that terrible deprivation!

image=/vmlinux.gz
        label=oops
        root=/dev/sda2
        append=""

Put this into a file 'etc/milo.conf' on **that partition** on which your
"image" file happens to live.  Now you have seen one.

You may put into a string assigned to 'append' string all other
arguments you may need to boot (multiple ethernet cards setup, scsi
controller parameters, whatever...).

You may have multiple stanzas like the one above; better with different
labels, of course. :-)

: I know the following works: MILO> boot sda1:vmlinux.gz
: root=/dev/sda2

So now try to boot from a milo prompt:  'b sda1:' and see what
transpires.  Unfortunately I have not seen a milo yet for which
'timeout=...' for an automatic booting a default entry would work.

There is another catch.  If /dev/sda1 happens to be a FAT partition
then 'etc/milo.conf' there will be seen by milo as 'etc/milo.con'
(an infamous 8+3 and milo knows nothing about vfat) so it will be
not found without hacking milo (entirely possible).  With iso9660
we have the same problem so a name like 'milo.cnf' would be,
in a retrospect, nicer.

  Michal




