On Thu, 6 Jan 2000 11:35:16 -0800, "Peter Willis"
<peterw@coastnet.com> wrote:


>The problem I am having is with MS visual C++ and a small console program
>that I am running.
>
>On the alpha microprocessors how do I keep the boolean comparison of two
>double precision floating point numbers from generating an exception?
>
>Why would this generate an illegal floating point operation exception.
>
>ie:
>double a,b;
>
>if(a > b) /*exception is generated at this point*/

Most likely, one of a or b is not a valid floating point value.  It
could be an exceptional value or a denorm - by default, these are
considered invalid.  I know that in Compaq's compilers there is an
option to allow these types of values to be used, but I don't know
offhand what it is in MSVC (which reminds me that you posted in a
Linux newsgroup, and MSVC doesn't run there...)

The most likely situation is that a or b is uninitialized or you have
an error in argument passing.


