>faulty. 
>
>Is there any info about this or anyplace I can get a Multia hardware
>reference guide to check jumpers or something?

I've solved it. And you guys all helped out. 

There were 2 problems. The primary problem was the no video problem.
The cause was indeed with the SIMMs.  I took a look at a good Multia
troubleshooting FAQ on Starship's web site (they sold me the Multias).

http://www.starshipcorp.com/troubleshooting.htm

If you count the blinks of the ethernet LED, you can determine what's
causing the problem. In my case, it blinked 14 times, which indicated
a RAM problem. I replaced the SIMMs with some other full parity ones
and both my machines came up nicely.

One of them was still exibiting some very strange behavior: reporting
a 125Mhz clock rather than 166Mhz and the inability to switch into ARC
(despite my attempts to perform a failsafe firmware install). This
problem was due to a dead battery. Removing the battery completely
allowed the machine to perform properly, and temporarily replacing it
with the other Multia's battery also performed properly. So I ordered
a couple new ones.

Thanks again...




