Steeve McCauley wrote:
>
> [convert dos style to bsd style partition tables?]
> 
> > The latter is
> > easy:
> > fdisk /dev/<disk>
> > p  (show current table)
> > write down partition ino
> > b  (BSD-style labeling)
> > recreate all partitions
> > w  (write)
> > q  (quit)
> > reboot
> 
> Seems straightforward enough, and that's what I would have
> tried but I didn't know whether the process of rewriting the
> partition tables would overwrite any information.  So do
> both the dos and bsd tables exist on the disk then?

I converted my partitions this way and all went fairly
well.  The one problem that I had on reboot was that
the first partition was originally /dev/sda2 but after converting
to bsd style it because partition 'a' which because /dev/sda1.

This shift cause several failed boots which
were repaired by booting to the RH 6.1 setup and mounting
the disk's root partiton and fixing fstab.  Finally, I had
to use the root=/dev/sda1 option to aboot successfully boot
since the second partition is assumed by aboot to be the
boot partition by aboot (this can be changed with the
command "abootconf /dev/sda 1").  Until that's done
I can boot using,

boot dka0 -file 1/boot/vmlinuz-2.2.13-0.9 -flags "/dev/sda1 single"

Thanks to everyone for the help -- even to the cranky
alpha experts who shall remain nameless ;)

