> I'm not against Alpha or something, the newer ones are great. A friend of mine
> has 600 Mhz Alpha, but it needs to be clear to new buyers that Multias and
> AXPpci33's need absurdly much memory to run smoothly and the IDE-controller on
> those motherboards are no good, while it's performance is not amazing. As I
> said before, 16 MB *is* reasonable for an Intel Linux box, 64 MB is really much
> for an x86 Linux box and certainly not the limit.

I'll second that, I've run X on 486 machines with 16M and a cheap S3 card
and never had a problem (not running apps at the same time but just using
it as an X server for running apps over a network).

> IDE hard disks are very fast on an x86 box.

From experimentation on a half dozen machines I've found that SCSI is
almost always faster but never much faster. At very best twice as fast,
usually 20% or so. However, SCSI use of DMA leaves a lot more CPU spare than
IDE use of polled-IO, the advantage that you get out of this will depend
on what else you might be doing while the drive access is happening -- with
interactive applications, probably not much.

I must admit I haven't gone through all the linux options on DMA on the IDE
drives, I'm sure that a good IDE controller running DMA will lighten the
CPU load considerably.

The only other good thing about SCSI is you can hang a lot of drives off
the one controller.

> If you can lend a Pentium from a friend, try Linux on it, you'll
> see that the performance is very satisfying. I bought the AXPpci33
> just because of curiosity and ran Linux on it for some time and as
> the HOWTO states, the AXPpci33 is a low-performance Alpha.

I found the same with the Multia, comparing it's 166MHz CPU on a 33MHz bus
against P150 machines on a 33MHz bus (and giving the Multia twice as much
memory) the Pentium was faster at most things, especially compilation.
I must say that the Multia does have a nive VGA output (using that TGA chipset
which seems to have a Brooktree stamp on it) -- very clear and steady running
X under Linux, even at high resolution, though limited to 256 colours and
not very fast at drawing. I could imagine that the Multia would make a 
reasonable machine to do line-art or word processing, maybe function plotting
in 2D, that sort of stuff.

> If I'd ever buy a non x86, I'd rather buy a Sun box or an SGI. I've used
> Sun boxes for four years now and they are really fast and versatile.

When you say ``really fast'', I've found them not much different from
Pentium machines for what I'm doing (which is finite element calculations).
Most of the machines seem to be limited by how fast you can pull data
out of memory, through the CPU and back into memory. When I compared a
Pentium running on a 66MHz backplane to a SPARC running on a 66MHz backplane
the speed was amazingly similar. When I tried a PowerPC running on a 100MHz
backplane it was 3/2 times the speed. The conclusion is that (for memory
intensive applications) you can forget the CPU and just worry about how
fast the memory can clock (also how wide the memory BUS is, most common
machines use 64bit memory, newer Alphas are much wider).

	- Tel




